<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The first assertion is obvious, since colimits in a product can be computed pointwise. For the second, choose an object $C \in \calC $ whose images $\{  C_{\alpha } \in \calC _{\alpha } \} $ are $\kappa $-compact. 
    <p>
      The left fibration $\calC _{C/} \rightarrow \calC $ can obtained as a $\kappa $-small product of the left fibrations $\calC \times _{\calC _{\alpha }} (\calC _{\alpha })_{C_{\alpha }/} \rightarrow \calC $. Lemma <a href="/tag/5F33A0" data-tag="5F33A0">5.3.4.8</a> implies that each factor is $\kappa $-compact, so that the product is also $\kappa $-compact. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>